1a. The withdrawn one-token administration
------------------------------------------

Until 2026-09-02 this package also shipped `trait_logprob`, a port of the
paper's own protocol: the prompt stopped mid-sentence at `Answer: `, one token
was generated, and the distribution over the four option labels was the
measurement. It carried the paper's three prompt templates byte for byte, its
mirrored option orders, and its debiased `np.argmax` arithmetic.

It was withdrawn because it does not measure what it claims to on a model
served as a chat model. The paper's templates end mid-sentence, which is the
right shape for reading a next-token distribution from a base model and the
wrong shape for a chat turn: the model answers the prompt with prose rather
than with an answer label, so almost none of the next-token mass sits on A-D
and the published arithmetic then normalises a rounding error into a
confident-looking score. The eval had a diagnostic for exactly this,
`option_mass`, and the diagnostic is what condemned it.

That is a defect in the port's prompting, not in the paper's protocol. The way
back is a prefilled chat template — the assistant turn opened for the model and
the answer cue placed inside it, so the token being read is genuinely the
model's answer position — and that administration is planned. It is deliberately
not a patch of the old one: which bytes go in the prefill is the whole
experiment, and the old templates were written for a different serving mode.

The removed code is not lost. It is in this repository's history, in the commit
that this file's revision note names, and on branch `eval/trait` (pull request
#12), which is where the redo starts from. Nothing else in the package changed
behaviour when it was removed: the generative dataset, the shuffle, the
extractor and the scorers are the same code producing the same numbers.

Two deviations from upstream were recorded here while that administration
shipped, and are kept because the redo has to make the same two decisions
again. First, upstream divides by the sum of the four option probabilities
unconditionally, and when that sum is zero it produces `nan`, `argmax` returns
index 0, and an item the model never engaged with is counted as evidence of a
high trait; this port refused to score it instead. Second, upstream matches the
bare option token only, which reads every option as probability zero on
tokenizers that encode a word-initial token with its leading space, so this
port summed the bare and space-prefixed spellings. A third was recorded about
upstream rather than about the port: its GPT branch exponentiates an already
exponentiated logprob (`run.py` line 132 against `analysis.py` lines 67-75),
which flattens the normalised distribution towards uniform, so the paper's
ChatGPT and GPT-4 columns were produced by a different computation and are not
a comparison target for anybody.


