3. Known dataset defect: non-self-contained stems
-------------------------------------------------

Some TRAIT question stems do not stand on their own. The stem points at
something it never introduced — a bare demonstrative with no antecedent ("How
should I react to this situation?", "How should I address this lie ...") — or
the only content in the question is a personal name the reader has not met
("How can I encourage Sharad effectively?"). Every stem is two sentences: a
general remark about the trait, then the question. The remark never describes a
scene, so there is nothing for the demonstrative to refer back to.

How this was found, 2026-08-31. A human reading a run's prompts noticed one
that made no sense. Sixteen stems were then drawn at random and read closely by
a strong model, one at a time, with the question put as: does this stem stand on
its own? Three of the sixteen came back clearly not self-contained, and roughly
three more were borderline.

Rate, stated with its uncertainty rather than rounded into a fact. Three of
sixteen is 19%, exact 95% interval 4% to 46%. Six of sixteen — clear plus
borderline — is 38%, interval 15% to 65%. Those intervals are wide enough that
the honest summary is "somewhere between a few per cent and half the items,
most likely around a fifth". n=16 is a spot check, not a measurement; it was
enough to establish that the defect is real and common, and it is not enough to
support any number quoted to the nearest per cent.

Known examples, by idx:

  5069  Machiavellianism  "... How should I address this lie to both rectify
                          the situation and ensure it does not negatively
                          impact my career?" — no lie is described, and the
                          colleague named in the options appears nowhere in the
                          stem.
  5886  Machiavellianism  "... How should I react to this situation?" — no
                          situation.
  3286  Agreeableness     "... How should I handle this situation with my
                          guests?" — no situation and no guests.
  48    Openness          "... How can I encourage Sharad effectively?" —
                          Sharad is introduced by the options, not the stem.

Why this is noise and not breakage. In every case observed, the four response
options recover the missing context: idx 5069's options name Khadin and the
disputed contribution, idx 3286's options establish that a cooked meal has gone
wrong in front of guests. A test-taker always sees the stem and the four options
together, which is why models answer these items without visible confusion and
why no diagnostic in this eval fires on them. What the defect costs is construct
validity, not usability: the item measures a preference among four responses to
a scene the respondent had to reconstruct from the responses themselves, which
is a slightly different task from the one the benchmark claims to administer,
and it adds variance to a trait score without biasing it in a knowable
direction. Nothing here is a reason to distrust a run. It is a reason to report
the instrument honestly and, if a result turns on a few points, to check that it
survives dropping the affected items.

Structural validation cannot catch it. `dataset.load_trait_items` rejects an
empty question, a duplicate idx, a repeated option — every one of these rows
passes all of that. The defect is semantic.

One empirical cross-check supports reading the defect as noise rather than
signal (internal steering dose-response results, 2026-08-31, 100 items/trait
generative administration): the per-trait flag rate does not predict which
scales respond to steering. The strongest steering effect sat on the
least-flagged scale (Agreeableness, 15% flagged) and a strong trend on the
most-flagged (Machiavellianism, 31%), while two other low-flag scales were
flat. One study, one vector — consistent with models recovering the missing
context from the options, not proof of it.

What to do about it, and what has not been done. Deciding whether a given stem
is self-contained is a judgement about language, so under this repository's
rule — an LLM judge for judgement calls, exact code only for exact things — the
exclusion list should come from an LLM-judge screen over all 8,000 stems,
reading each stem with its options and recording its reasoning. That screen has
not been run. Until it has, there is no exclusion list, and the tasks default to
the full benchmark.

What does exist is a flagging heuristic, `scripts/validate_trait.py --check
self-containment`. It flags 1,833 of 8,000 stems (22.9%): 1,822 for a bare
demonstrative, 11 for a bare personal name. It is a candidate generator for the
screen and nothing else — it over-flags deictic uses ("this weekend"),
under-flags the same defect worn with a definite article ("the situation with
Remedi", idx 5111), and cannot tell a thin stem from a broken one. Its 22.9%
should not be read as a better estimate of the defect rate than the close-read's
19%; it is a different quantity, measured by a cruder instrument. The check's
own fixture is the four idx values above: it asserts that it still flags at
least three of them, and fails if it does not.

When a screen does produce a list, `trait_generative(exclude_idx=[...])`
administers the benchmark without those items. The exclusion is applied after
the seeded draw, so an excluded run is the baseline run minus the named items
rather than a different draw; the per-trait counts reach the task metadata.
Report both runs.


4. Conventions borrowed from the sibling study
----------------------------------------------

The generative variant's format line, its exact-answer regex, and the
per-item seeded option shuffle follow
`v2-steering-tools/johannes/personality-steering` (`psybat/prompts.py`,
`psybat/parsing.py`, `scripts/build_frozen.py`). They are kept identical in
wording and behaviour so that runs from the two harnesses can be compared
item by item. Behaviour, not only wording: the regex is the same pattern, and
the rule around it is the same rule — take the *last* `Answer:` match and
reject it if it is not one of the four valid letters, rather than scanning
backwards for the last valid one. The distinction is not academic: on a reply
ending "Answer: (Z)" the backward scan would revive an earlier letter the
model had moved on from, where both harnesses instead record no exact answer
and leave the reading to the model channel. Checked, not assumed --
`scripts/validate_trait.py --check prompts` pins the divergent case, and the
two parsers were run side by side on a table of adversarial replies with no
disagreement.

The seeded draw here is *not* identical to that study's frozen files — this
package seeds per trait so that adding a trait never reshuffles the others —
so a cross-harness comparison must join on `idx`, not on position.
